INT. FOG SIGNAL STATION - NIGHT

A stone tower on a bald rock in a gray sea. A great iron horn mounted in the wall. The room is round, cramped, lit by a hanging oil lamp that sways though the air is still. The walls are slick with condensation. The floor has been scrubbed so many times the boards are white.

DAGNY HOLM (40s, keeper, oilskin coat, a wool cap pulled to her eyebrows, a thick log-book in her lap) sits at a table with a mug gone cold. PEREGRINE LOOMIS (20s, apprentice, glasses fogged, jacket too big for him) stands at the narrow window with a telescope. SOL (60s, deaf, white beard, one milky eye) rocks in a chair by the iron stove, hands knotted around a pipe.

The horn sounds. A single, mournful bellow. The lamp shivers. Everyone waits for the echo.

None comes.

PEREGRINE
Something's out there.

DAGNY
It's fog.

PEREGRINE
It's moving against the fog.

DAGNY
Fog moves.

PEREGRINE
Not like this. Come see.

She doesn't. She turns a page in the log. Sol rocks. The chair creaks. The pipe cools.

PEREGRINE (CONT'D)
Dagny. It has lights.

DAGNY
A ship, then.

PEREGRINE
Seven lights. In a row. Low on the water.

DAGNY
Count again.

PEREGRINE
Eight.

Dagny stands. She goes to the window, takes the telescope from his hands, puts it to her eye. A long silence. The lamp swings once.

DAGNY
Ten.

PEREGRINE
They're getting closer.

Sol stops rocking. He stands, and the chair keeps moving without him. He crosses to the horn mechanism and puts a hand flat on the iron, feels the tremor.

SOL
(gravelly)
They're answering it.

DAGNY
Sol.

SOL
It sounds back from the water. I felt it in my bones.

Peregrine takes the telescope back and sweeps the sea. Dagny seizes the horn lever. Sol grips her wrist. They stare at each other across a foot of cold air, and between them the lamp swings and the shadows swing with it.

PEREGRINE
(whisper)
Don't sound it.

DAGNY
If I don't, the ships hit the rock.

SOL
If you do, those ones come in.

DAGNY
Which ones?

The sea gives a long, low, rolling moan. The iron horn, silent, answers it, humming in the wall like a plucked string. From the dark down the stairwell comes a wet sound, one step, then another, climbing.

Peregrine backs away from the window. Dagny releases the lever. Sol raises both hands and pulls the cap from his head, as if it were a church.

The footsteps stop at the door.

A knock, polite.

Dagny, Sol, Peregrine look at each other, the same thought in all three faces. Then, from the other side of the wood, a voice, thin, patient, exactly like Peregrine's.

PEREGRINE (V.O.)
(through the door)
Dagny? It's me. Let me in.

Peregrine, at the window, has not moved his lips.
